% ============================================================================
% season-start-month-number — which NUMBERED month each meteorological season
% STARTS in, DERIVED (not looked up) by bridging a season fact to a calendar
% fact (adj-facts-stdlib, EARTH-SCIENCE, cross-directory composition with
% CALENDAR). NGSS 1-ESS1-2 (observing seasonal patterns across the year).
% ============================================================================
% `earth-science/seasons.adj` already tables each season's START MONTH as a
% NAME ("summer" starts in "june"); `calendar/months.adj` already tables each
% month NAME's 1-based NUMBER ("june" is month 6). Neither file states outright
% "summer starts in month 6" — but composing the two ALREADY-SHIPPED facts
% answers it directly, exactly the bridge `seasons.adj`'s own header comment
% already invites ("to turn it into a number for arithmetic, compose it with
% the calendar library... the concrete bridge from RECALL to COMPUTE"). This is
% the SAME "compose two independently-citable facts into a derived, dual-cited
% conclusion" discipline `language/word-families.adj` and
% `physics/heat-causes-phase-change.adj` already established — applied here to
% a genuinely CROSS-DIRECTORY pair (earth-science + calendar, not two files in
% the same subject directory like those two precedents).
%
%     import "season-start-month-number.adj"
%     ? season_start_month_number(summer, $N)   % 6
%     ? season_start_month_number($S, 12)       % winter (reverse recall)
%     ? season_start_month_number(monsoon, $N)  % abstain -- not a tabled season
%
% This is a native `rule` (ADJ-TABLES RS-6): its body binds `$Season`/`$Month`
% through the already-shipped `season_start_month` table, then re-binds
% `$Month`/`$Number` through the already-shipped `month_number` table -- a
% plain two-hop join, not a causal inference (unlike `causes_phase_change` or
% `force_causes_acceleration`, this rule states no NEW principle of its own;
% it just names the standard calendar convention that a month has both a NAME
% and a NUMBER). A query's `steps` trail shows THREE entries: the rule's own
% citation (the calendar-numbering convention), the `season_start_month` fact's
% citation (NOAA), and the `month_number` fact's citation (ISO 8601), so a
% human can independently verify each link. Honest abstention on any season
% word `seasons.adj` does not table (an equinox, a solstice, a made-up
% "monsoon") -- the rule only derives what its premises can ground.
%
% CROSS-DIRECTORY IMPORT: this file lives in `earth-science/` (its natural
% subject home, alongside `seasons.adj`) and imports its calendar sibling with
% a relative `../calendar/months.adj` path -- empirically confirmed (in a
% scratch dir, before writing this file) that this resolves cleanly PROVIDED
% the consuming entry program lives at or above the package root (so both
% `earth-science/` and `calendar/` fall inside its sandbox subtree; the CLI's
% import sandbox is rooted at the TOP-LEVEL PROGRAM's own directory, not each
% importer's directory -- confirmed by reading `adj-lang-cli`'s `FsProvider`
% source directly, not just guessing from the error message). This is the
% SAME shape `mathematics/word-problems.adj` already established (it lives in
% `mathematics/` and imports `../arithmetic/arithmetic.adj`); mirroring that
% precedent, this library's OWN `.query.adj` companion is placed at the
% package root (not beside this file) so a bare `adj-lang-cli
% <query>.query.adj` invocation from the package root works directly.
%
% Deliberately scoped to the STANDARD calendar-number bridge only (Northern-
% Hemisphere meteorological seasons, inherited unchanged from `seasons.adj`'s
% own scope note) -- no new season or month content is asserted here, only
% the join. (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

import "seasons.adj"
import "../calendar/months.adj"

rule { head: season_start_month_number($Season, $Number)
       when: season_start_month($Season, $Month), month_number($Month, $Number)
       source "MM is the month of the year between 01 (January) and 12 (December)"
       locator "https://www.cl.cam.ac.uk/~mgk25/iso-time.html"
       trust consensus }
